is massive training  set  of high resolution flow fields to train  on so this data is from the johns  hopkins turbulence database it's a  fantastic resource  where you can download tons and tons and  tons of high fidelity  data from fluid simulations a variety of  fluid simulations  huge service to the field uh is the  johns hopkins turbulence data set  and so what ben showed was that  essentially  you can get this really really nice  super resolution  uh reconstruction of your image even  from this very low resolution down  sample  image if you run this as an  interpolation  and this is this is what's i think  really important is in machine learning  you know that when you train your  algorithms you take your your data set  and often what you do is you kind of  randomly pull eighty percent of that  data set to train your model  and then you test it on the remaining  twenty percent that's and then you would  randomly reshuffle and do it again and  again and again  in a cross validation okay and if we do  that  we actually get excellent excellent  reconstruction super resolution  reconstructions  and remember this came from a movie  these image is our image sequences from  a movie  and so if i look down here this kind of  standard cross-validation approach  is essentially an interpolation problem  so these uh what we do is we train on  these dark columns  and we test on these bright blue columns  and so this bright blue column here is  sandwiched right between two images  in my training data so in some sense  we're able to  do this reconstruction because it's an  interpolation  of things i've already seen in the flow  field okay  when we do an extrapolation task things  get much much trickier and this is  actually more realistic for lots of flow  scenarios  maybe i have this movie and i train for  the first eighty percent of it in time  and then i try to predict or do the  super resolution on the next twenty  percent in time  so i'm not randomly sampling  in time but i only sample the first 80  this is what you would actually want to  do if you for example wanted to build a  model  uh of the climate or the weather or the  flow in the ocean as you do  historical data and then you would start  applying that model  in the future starting at time now okay  and that's a much harder problem that's  an extrapolation problem so the farther  you get from  where your training data ends if you  start you know the first snapshot right  after  you get beautiful reconstruction but as  you go farther and farther  in time away from where you had training  data your estimation degrades  and so extrapolation is super hard you  have to do a lot more you have to build  in physics if you want to be able to do  a proper extrapolation  task at least in a lot of cases and in  image sciences i want to point out  when you train uh like a classifier or  something  a super resolution on imagenet the  assumption  is is that that training data has  pretty much everything you'd ever want  to classify in it  so it already contains everything that  you ever would want to to reconstruct or  classify  and the assumption is that all future  tasks are going to be an interpolation  of that training data that is not the  case in lots of fluid systems it is not  possible to just interpolate uh these  flow fields  in the future and that's one of the key  distinctions is that these flow fields  these turbulent flow fields often have  many many ranges of scale separation in  space  and in time that make it very hard to  generalize to future scenarios  and so this is kind of a an example of  lots of flow fields this is a great  paper by  by jared callahan you can read it about  it in physical review fluids or on the  archive  and what he looked at was this range of  flow fields of varying complexity  and kind of how a training data set  would generalize to a testing data set  so often times we build our models and  demonstrate our algorithms on this  really simple flow past a cylinder  in a lot of ways i think what jared  showed is that that's  far too simple of an example and when  you start using those ideas in these  really complicated flow fields like the  the flow in the gulf of mexico or this  spatially evolving mixing layer flow you  really have a hard time  getting your training data to have  enough kind of richness of data  that it will generalize to the future  and so  you can actually kind of quantify this  at least using the singular value  decomposition  how much training data you need before  you get a certain  accuracy kind of reconstruction in the  future for an extrapolation task  and essentially what what he shows here  is that for some of these flow fields  you might need tens of thousands or  hundreds of thousands of snapshots  before you reach this kind of  statistical stationarity where you get  an interpolation problem instead of an  extrapolation problem  and these are not actually even that  complex of flow fields right like  industrial flow fields flow past a  submarine or a boeing jet  are going to be exceedingly high  dimensional and they're going to be even  harder to characterize in the  statistically stationary way  okay good so i think that's really  really kind of neat to think about  now again that was just using the  standard linear proper orthogonal  decomposition  you might be able to get better  representations with these deep  autoencoders  people are working on it this is an  active area of research  okay so maybe as i conclude i'll just  give you kind of this  this you know slightly light-hearted  example so this is one of my favorite  um images is this this painting um  la primavera by archimbaldo and i  believe this is a painting of emperor  rudolph ii  where he would essentially represent  famous people as flowers or vegetables  or fruits  and this was all done in archimbaldo's  imagination this was hundreds of years  ago  but it's a lot like the modern deep  dreams that we see now that are so  popular  today and so this is a really cool image  visualization where someone has taken  uh archimbaldo's essentially deep dream  and they have repainted  it with other features like peacock  feathers or dogs or cats  and so i think this is really kind of  meta that it's a deep dream  of one of the original deep dreams but  this begs the question  if i have a flow field again like my  reynolds number 100 cylinder flow  can i do a deep dream can i paint this  with the texture of a turbulent flow  or of starry night right can i take a  simple flow that's easy to characterize  easy to model easy to control and can i  in a physically relevant way  paint it so that it looks like a much  more turbulent uh or complex flow field  okay so uh lots of interesting stuff  again this is the tip of the iceberg but  if you can treat your fluid flow  like an image sequence you can leverage  tons of powerful techniques for machine  learning  and again i think we're only scratching  the the surface of what can be done here  there's tons more to do  uh read more about it get into it it's  going to be a super exciting  field for decades to come all right  thank you
 

